#!/bin/sh
# Quillex release boot environment
# =================================
#
# What is this file?
# ------------------
# This is an EEx *template*, not a script that is run from the source tree.
# When `MIX_ENV=prod mix release` assembles Quillex, Mix evaluates this file
# and copies the result into the release as:
#
#     releases/<quillex-version>/env.sh
#
# The release launcher sources that generated shell script before every
# `bin/quillex` command, and before the BEAM starts. Environment variables
# exported here therefore configure the release launcher and the Erlang VM;
# this is earlier and lower-level than Quillex application configuration.
#
# The `.eex` suffix means the template may use release metadata such as
# `<%%= @release.name %>`. This template currently needs no interpolation,
# but keeping the standard Mix filename lets `mix release` discover it.
#
# Official explainers:
#
#   * Elixir's release configuration guide (start with “Operating system
#     environment configuration”):
#     https://hexdocs.pm/elixir/config-and-releases.html#operating-system-environment-configuration
#
#   * `mix release`, including the generated directory layout and every
#     supported `RELEASE_*` environment variable:
#     https://hexdocs.pm/mix/Mix.Tasks.Release.html#module-environment-variables
#
# What does Quillex set here?
# ---------------------------
# Mix releases use short-name Erlang distribution (`sname`) by default.
# Distribution gives release operators facilities such as:
#
#     bin/quillex remote
#     bin/quillex rpc 'Some.Module.function()'
#
# It also turns the desktop editor into a named Erlang node and normally
# starts/contacts EPMD, Erlang's node-name mapper. EPMD listens on TCP port
# 4369. Quillex is a local GUI application, not a node in an Erlang cluster,
# so keeping distribution enabled would expose machinery the product neither
# needs nor intends to support.
#
# `RELEASE_DISTRIBUTION` accepts `sname`, `name`, or `none`; `none` tells the
# generated launcher not to start distribution automatically. This setting
# does NOT disable file watching, Scenic input, ordinary application network
# clients, or any of Quillex's three application start modes. It affects only
# Erlang node distribution and the release commands that depend on it.
#
# Erlang's EPMD reference:
# https://www.erlang.org/doc/apps/erts/epmd_cmd.html
#
# Trade-off: `remote` and `rpc` cannot attach to a running Quillex release.
# For an interactive development session, use
# `MIX_ENV=dev qlx`, which runs from source with development tooling attached.
export RELEASE_DISTRIBUTION=none
